  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Response to emergency caused by floods in Choco Department, Colombia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The emergency started with the overflowing and flooding of rivers on the 18th October 2016 affecting 20 municipalities in Choc&#xf3; Department, generating loss of crops, homes, and livelihoods of more than 17,000 families. The heavy rains in early to mid November 2016, some of the strongest in recent history, have aggravated the original situation in those municipalities and affected an additional municipality of Bahia Solano with 1,600 families.  While the waters have gone down slightly from original levels, most areas remain flooded, and the pre-existing conditions of extreme poverty and vulnerability, lack of state services and armed conflict in the region, have aggravated the emergency situation. Choc&#xf3; is Colombia&#x2019;s poorest department. It faces serious challenges in terms of governance and a lack of state presence (for example around health and education), so in general the local government&#x2019;s capacity to respond to emergencies is not very strong.</narrative>
  </description>
